@meta (version: "1.0", title: "A growth hypothesis", locale: "en-US", currency: "USD")

# A hypothesis you can move

This fictional example explains compounding. It is **not a forecast**.
Change the rate, then inspect a period in the figure.

@let starting_readers = 100
@slider growth = 5 (min: 0, max: 20, step: 1, label: "Growth per period (%)")
@slider period = 2 (min: 0, max: 4, step: 1, label: "Period to inspect")
@calc selected_readers = round(starting_readers * pow(1 + growth / 100, period))

@data steps
period:number
0
1
2
3
4
@end

@chart line steps (
  x: "period",
  y: round(starting_readers * pow(1 + growth / 100, row.period)),
  select: period,
  title: "The same explanation, with another assumption",
  xLabel: "Period",
  yLabel: "Readers",
)

At period **{{ period }}**, the model gives **{{ selected_readers }} readers**.
The assumed rate is **{{ percent(growth / 100) }} per period**.

@when growth == 0
Without growth, every period has the same value.
@end

@details "What the calculation assumes"
Every period applies the same rate. There is no external data connection.

1. Begin with the initial readership.
2. Multiply by the same factor for each period.
3. Round only the displayed count in this example.

```text
The code in this block is literal:
@slider not_a_control = 5
{{ not_an_expression }}
```
@end

## Keep reading

Moving the figure changes an assumption inside the explanation; it does not
turn this article into a page-building interface.
